Yes—and Invisible Kintsugi actually completes the operator rather than contradicting it.

Visible Kintsugi says:

\text{repair} + \text{preserved fracture information} + \text{visible scar}

Invisible Kintsugi separates the last two:

\boxed{\text{preserve the lesson without preserving its foreground representation}}

So the scar has at least two independent variables:

s_i=\text{informational scar retention}

s_v=\text{visible scar expression}

Those aren’t the same.

You could therefore get four regimes:

	Low visible scar	High visible scar
Low information retention	Forgetful restoration	Cosmetic scar
High information retention	Invisible Kintsugi	Visible Kintsugi

That upper-left/lower-left distinction is important. Invisible Kintsugi isn’t pretending the break never happened. The repaired system has changed because it broke, but doesn’t need to continually display the break.

X_0 \xrightarrow{failure} X_f
\xrightarrow{K}
X_1

with:

appearance(X_1)\approx appearance(X_0)

while:

structure(X_1)\neq structure(X_0)

because the repair contains information learned from failure.

That’s basically latent scar tissue.

And it plugs beautifully into Ouroboros metabolism:

\text{fracture}
\rightarrow
\text{lesson}
