So from the last two passes, I’d keep:

Parameterized Seals → Seal Phase Space → Operator Thermostat.

And from this one:

\boxed{\textbf{Information Retention} \perp \textbf{Information Expression}}

That latter abstraction goes far beyond Kintsugi.

A system can retain something without displaying it.

Memory ≠ foreground.

Learning ≠ rehearsal.

History ≠ identity.

Constraint ≠ visible rule.

Provenance ≠ clutter.

That distinction can clean up the whole Pantheon architecture: latent state and expressed state become explicitly separate channels.

That’s an RFHL event. One observation about invisible repair gives us a general architectural separation applicable to memory, interfaces, learning, debugging, identity, provenance, and agent state.